WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.590 --> 00:00:04.550
For years, viewers have turned to this hit
reality series for their fix of sweet bikes

00:00:04.550 --> 00:00:05.930
and family fights.

00:00:05.930 --> 00:00:10.130
But if you lost track of the American Chopper
stars during their long hiatus, don't worry.

00:00:10.130 --> 00:00:13.560
We've got you covered with this look at everything
they've been up to.

00:00:13.560 --> 00:00:19.000
Just a day before the American Chopper reboot
premiered in March 2018, Paul Sr. filed for

00:00:19.030 --> 00:00:20.030
bankruptcy.

00:00:20.030 --> 00:00:24.650
According to paperwork obtained by Page Six,
Teutul admitted to owing nearly 50 creditors

00:00:24.650 --> 00:00:29.869
a total of more than $1 million, against a
net worth of around $1.8 million.

00:00:29.869 --> 00:00:36.890
Teutul also claimed a monthly income of $15,000
and expenditures of just over $12,600 not

00:00:36.890 --> 00:00:38.770
enough to pay off his crushing debts.

00:00:38.770 --> 00:00:43.760
"My goal was to build maybe 5, 10 bikes a
year.

00:00:43.760 --> 00:00:48.280
More for the hobby and passion than for making
money"

00:00:48.280 --> 00:00:49.880
But the story wasn't over.

00:00:49.880 --> 00:00:55.270
In April 2019, the trustee in charge of Teutul's
case asked the court to dismiss the bankruptcy

00:00:55.270 --> 00:00:56.270
petition.

00:00:56.270 --> 00:01:00.100
According to that filing, Teutul didn't provide
the trustee with a copy of a federal income

00:01:00.100 --> 00:01:06.560
tax return or transcript for 2017 and 2018,
and more proof that his financial situation,

00:01:06.560 --> 00:01:08.999
quote, "indicates a negative cash flow."

00:01:08.999 --> 00:01:12.709
Four months before he filed for bankruptcy
and the whole world suddenly knew about his

00:01:12.709 --> 00:01:17.049
daunting financial problems, Paul Sr. put
his enormous Montgomery, New York estate on

00:01:17.049 --> 00:01:19.240
the market for $2.9 million.

00:01:19.240 --> 00:01:22.799
Teutul then cut the price to $2.49 million.

00:01:22.799 --> 00:01:27.149
And by early 2019, it was down to just $1.65
million.

00:01:27.149 --> 00:01:30.979
The trustee in charge of his bankruptcy case
said Teutul seemed sure that selling his home

00:01:30.979 --> 00:01:35.039
would solve all his money woes, but it doesn't
seem to have worked out that way.

00:01:35.040 --> 00:01:36.840
At least, not yet.

00:01:36.840 --> 00:01:40.590
All those fights between Senior and Junior
were apparently real.

00:01:40.590 --> 00:01:45.779
Tensions ran so high at Orange County Choppers
that in 2008, Paul Sr. fired Jr. from the

00:01:45.779 --> 00:01:47.749
shop, even though he co-owned it.

00:01:47.749 --> 00:01:52.939
Junior later told Fox News that his dad giving
him the axe was what he called "a big favor"

00:01:52.939 --> 00:01:55.689
because it set him on the path to starting
his own business.

00:01:55.689 --> 00:01:56.689
Paul Jr.

00:01:56.689 --> 00:01:58.400
Designs opened in 2010.

00:01:58.400 --> 00:02:02.689
"The beauty of it is, you can get a history
lesson, and you can see all these bikes you've

00:02:02.689 --> 00:02:05.350
watched us build on the show for all these
years"

00:02:05.350 --> 00:02:10.590
Paul Sr. then tried to legally force Jr. to
sell him his 20 percent share of their company.

00:02:10.590 --> 00:02:14.069
But it turned out that getting rid of Junior
represented a breach of contract with the

00:02:14.069 --> 00:02:17.280
Discovery Channel, and could have led to the
show's cancellation.

00:02:17.280 --> 00:02:21.969
This all led the network to launch the spinoff,
American Chopper: Senior vs. Junior.

00:02:21.969 --> 00:02:25.959
After that series wrapped in 2012, Jr. went
back to Paul Jr.

00:02:25.960 --> 00:02:31.720
Designs, and in 2015 celebrated the birth
of his first child a son named Hudson.

00:02:31.760 --> 00:02:35.880
American Chopper fans came for the bikes but
stayed for the fights.

00:02:35.900 --> 00:02:40.239
But fabricator and mechanic Vinnie DiMartino
tried to keep his head down and get to work.

00:02:40.239 --> 00:02:45.250
In 2007, he left the Chopper franchise, and
founded his own shop, VForceCustoms.

00:02:45.250 --> 00:02:47.099
He wrote on the company's now defunct site,

00:02:47.099 --> 00:02:49.260
"I had gone as far as I could there.

00:02:49.260 --> 00:02:53.489
I really didn't have any chance for advancement,
and I had always wanted to have my own shop,

00:02:53.489 --> 00:02:56.490
so the natural progression was to leave and
start my own place."

00:02:56.490 --> 00:03:01.200
"Everybody thinks I'm a die hard biker, I'm
really not"

00:03:01.200 --> 00:03:07.290
In 2013, DiMartino abandoned bikes for cars
and opened DiMartino Motorsports, a car and

00:03:07.290 --> 00:03:09.960
truck repair company in Walden, New York.

00:03:09.960 --> 00:03:13.790
Rick Petko seemed like an even-keeled guy
on American Chopper.

00:03:13.790 --> 00:03:17.819
Even when the two Teutuls split up to run
separate shops, Petko stayed on with Senior

00:03:17.819 --> 00:03:19.290
but stayed amiable with Junior.

00:03:19.290 --> 00:03:22.650
Both Teutuls attended his wedding in 2012.

00:03:22.650 --> 00:03:25.849
And by the end of 2015, Petko was a dad to
two girls.

00:03:25.849 --> 00:03:29.939
He was still working at Orange County Choppers
by then, but, tired of his 90-minute commute

00:03:29.939 --> 00:03:33.879
each way from his home in Pennsylvania to
the shop in New York, he took a job as the

00:03:33.879 --> 00:03:39.200
chief fabricator at the Pocono Mountain Harley-Davidson
complex in Pennsylvania, roughly a mile from

00:03:39.200 --> 00:03:40.200
his house.

00:03:40.200 --> 00:03:44.350
"To run these bikes that are a hundred years
old, even though you're redoing them, you're

00:03:44.350 --> 00:03:49.060
adding history to them…it's something cool
about it"

00:03:49.060 --> 00:03:52.170
When he's not working with metal, Petko…
still works with metal.

00:03:52.170 --> 00:03:56.400
He runs a small knife-forging company out
of his home called RPD &amp; Co.

00:03:56.400 --> 00:04:01.310
Paul Sr. and Jr. frequently disagreed, but
fortunately, there was a third member of the

00:04:01.310 --> 00:04:04.189
Teutul family around to maintain the peace:
Mikey Teutul.

00:04:04.189 --> 00:04:07.109
But he walked away from the Chopper franchise
in 2012, saying,

00:04:07.109 --> 00:04:11.790
"[I want to] seek some peace of mind and work
on my relationship with my dad."

00:04:11.790 --> 00:04:14.260
"Ready on set"

00:04:14.260 --> 00:04:18.130
He later came back to work, but Teutul used
part of his time off to open an art gallery

00:04:18.130 --> 00:04:20.959
in Montgomery, New York, which featured his
paintings.

00:04:20.959 --> 00:04:25.770
It closed in 2014, but Teutul pursued another
artistic project: a web series.

00:04:25.770 --> 00:04:31.400
"Folks, this is Bob the Bum again talking
to you right here on Bum Television"

00:04:31.400 --> 00:04:37.470
Launched in 2016, Bummin' Around aims to shine
a light on New York City's homeless problem.

